@node D-Bus Overview
@section Overview

@subsection What is the cmacs D-Bus subsystem

cmacs registers as a session-bus service the moment
@code{cmacs-dbus-start} runs (this is automatic when @code{M-x bacon}
launches, and can be invoked explicitly from elisp).  Once running,
it carries:

@itemize @bullet
@item
A typed, introspectable method surface at @code{/org/cmacs/Editor}
@item
Standards-compliant ObjectManager and Properties interfaces (the
Properties iface attaches lazily as Phase 2 modules register state)
@item
Signal emission so external watchers get pushed updates instead of
having to poll
@item
D-Bus activation on demand: a fresh session can launch a cmacs daemon
by calling any method on @code{org.cmacs.Editor}
@end itemize

@subsection When to use D-Bus vs the alternatives

cmacs offers three IPC mechanisms.  Pick by transport:

@multitable @columnfractions .55 .45
@headitem Use case @tab Recommended IPC
@item Bacon shell child of a running cmacs
@tab @env{CMACS_IPC_FD} socketpair (already wired by cmacsgi)
@item Same-machine external script that doesn't care which cmacs
@tab D-Bus + well-known name @code{org.cmacs.Editor}
@item Targeting a specific cmacs process when several run in parallel
@tab D-Bus + per-PID name @code{org.cmacs.Editor.Pid@var{N}}
@item Editor-aware tooling that wants types, properties, signals
@tab D-Bus
@item Existing emacsclient-style ``open this file''
@tab @command{emacsclient} still works; D-Bus
@code{Application.Open} covers the same case once Phase 4 lands
@item AI agent / MCP integration
@tab MCP (separate channel, not D-Bus)
@end multitable

D-Bus is the @emph{external} path; cmacsgi via FD is the in-process
child path.  Both ultimately funnel through the same elisp
dispatcher, so behaviour is consistent.

@subsection Phase 1: what's actually shipping

After @code{cmacs-dbus-start}, you get:

@itemize @bullet
@item
Two bus names: well-known @code{org.cmacs.Editor} (claimed by the
first cmacs) and per-PID @code{org.cmacs.Editor.Pid@var{N}} (always
claimed)
@item
One root object path: @code{/org/cmacs/Editor}
@item
Interfaces on that path:
@code{org.freedesktop.DBus.Introspectable} (auto),
@code{org.freedesktop.DBus.Peer} (auto),
@code{org.freedesktop.DBus.ObjectManager} (registered, empty children),
@code{org.cmacs.Editor1} (back-compat: 6 core + 18 gowl methods),
@code{org.freedesktop.DBus.Properties} (lazy attach for Phase 2)
@item
Signal-emit C and Lisp APIs (no automatic emission yet --- Phase 2
hooks will start firing them)
@item
@file{.service} activation file at @file{/usr/share/dbus-1/services/}
(when @command{make install-cmacs-dbus-service} is run)
@end itemize

@subsection Architecture in one paragraph

The D-Bus subsystem lives in @file{cmacs/dbus/} as eight small files
(one top-level, five infrastructure modules, two interface modules).
All calls run on the cmacs main thread via the GMainContext ---
handlers fire from the same dispatch loop as GLib timeouts and idle
callbacks, so they can safely call Emacs primitives.  Method bodies
build elisp strings and route through the existing
@code{cmacs_dispatch_eval} helper, so the typed D-Bus surface and
the cmacsgi command surface share their semantics by construction.
See @ref{D-Bus Architecture}.
